00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:07.450 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  despite being the eighth installment in  a long-running series the newest god of  war is a beast of its own it continues  the storyline of its predecessors but  revamps their combat pacing and flow it  takes their linear structure and morphs  it into an open-ended world and although  god of war effectively iterates on the  franchise's own identity it borrows from  many outside sources as well some of  which are more surprising than you might  think  Metroid and Castlevania Symphony of the  Night have inspired a multitude of games  this sub-genre affectionately called  Metroidvania x' features interconnected  worlds in which certain paths are  blocked until you unlock specific  abilities through backtracking and  exploration these games can integrate  story and gameplay into a much closer  relationship I recently spoke with Corey  Barr log gotta Wars creative director  over the phone and he mentioned Symphony  of the Night as one of the chief  influences throughout his development  career it's like the closest perfection  for me as a gamer the beginning of the  game setting you up to lose everything  and desire to get back and get it back  better than ever that is resonated and  stuck with me for so long that it  influences everything that I do so you  know like the metroidvania design a lot  to be is something that I think is  deeply rooted in the way that I approach  all problem God of War is full of  enticing dead ends and secrets just out  of reach as in Metroidvania as you're  learning new layers of god of war at a  steady pace each new ability allows you  to reevaluate the world you traverse  every area again seeing your  surroundings through a new lens certain  puzzles now make sense old enemies  reveal new weaknesses whole side areas  open up beckoning you forward teasing  new pathways that may or may not require  further learning to progress it's one of  those rare games that makes backtracking  fun another recent third-person action  game put the metroidvania formula to  good use  2015's rise of the Tomb Raider uses  similar item gating to funnel you  through its world in fact it does so  almost right away pitting you against a  vicious grizzly bear only letting you  get by it once you learn to craft poison  arrows once past the bear a whole new  sandbox area opens up cue the  exploration rise of the Tomb Raider and  God of War have more in common than this  overall structure though bar log was  lead animator on God of War and Game  Director for God of War 2 he worked on  God of War 3 for nine months before  leaving Sony Santa Monica for a new  project that project was the 2013 Tomb  Raider reboot this game also uses a  light  Metroidvania approach morphing the  linear legacy of Tomb Raider  into one of sandbox level design two  years later Metroidvania aspects became  even more prevalent in the sequel  subsequently several of rise of the tomb  Raider's designers moved over to God of  War during the latter's creation because  of this there's a fair amount of osmosis  between the two projects as bar walk  says the video game industry is one big  melting pot of ideas games that bleed  into other games projects that iterate  on those that came before them if you  dig deep enough into any new release  you'll find the DNA of dozens of other  games but there are structural  similarities between rise of the Tomb  Raider and God of War  like the Crystal Dynamics project god of  war makes use of a massive hub  connecting smaller side areas each time  you return to the lake of nine you have  more abilities to use expanding the  world with new areas and reinforcing  your knowledge of existing ones it  brings you closer to the world and the  characters you're spending so much time  with and speaking of characters that  brings us to the Last of Us bar log has  spoken extensively about Joel and  Ellie's influence on his game the  relationship between Naughty Dog's  iconic duo is the centerpiece of the  Last of Us and the dynamic between  Kratos and his son is a pillar of God of  War  their journeys affect them not just as  individuals but also as teams that grow  alongside each other they fall down they  pick each other back up in speaking with  bar log there are numerous inspirations  that help god of war take shape some of  them lie outside video games such as the  film's rope Birdman or hard-boiled  movies that make use of long takes to  bring in immediacy to the ongoing story  then there are characters such as Quint  from Jaws or Adrian Monk who inspired  some of god of wars more colorful  personalities and of course there's the  Norse mythology that fuels the series  new narrative but in the end God of War  is a thing of its own separate not just  from its outside inspirations but from  its franchise predecessors as well it  brings an array of new mechanics to the  table it functions as a sequel to the  older God of War games and it works as a  beginning to a new saga it is more than  just the sum of its parts  once the credits have finally rolled  it's pretty clear God of War may have  began on the shoulders of giants but now  it's become a giant of its own  [Music]  you  [Music]

